From e24cf9001c0b3d28ba68c33a55b083e4fce02fd4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 7 Jun 2026 08:09:55 -0700
Subject: [PATCH RFC 0/3] net: flow_dissector: opt-in fast-path for common L3+L4 shapes

A small targeted optimisation to __skb_flow_dissect(). Two
fast-paths are added — one for eth+IPv4+{TCP,UDP}, one for
eth+IPv6+{TCP,UDP} — that collapse the slow-path's per-layer
parse-graph traversal (function-pointer dispatch + linear
lookup_node per layer, three layers per packet) into a single
straight-line block. On any miss, falls back to the existing
graph walk. Output is byte-identical to the slow path for the
shapes handled.

The fast-path is gated by a new sysctl, default off:

    net.core.flow_dissector_fastpath = 0   (default; no change)
    net.core.flow_dissector_fastpath = 1   (fast-path enabled)

The gate uses a static_branch_likely + proc_do_static_key (the
canonical helper for sysctl-toggled static_branches; cf.
net.core.high_order_alloc_disable). When the sysctl is 0 the
call-site test is patched to a forward JMP that skips the
dispatcher — one not-taken JMP per dissector call. When the
sysctl is 1 execution falls through inline to the dispatcher,
keeping the fast-path body in the hot text section.

  - What this does. Adds an opt-in fast-path for
    eth+IPv4+{TCP,UDP} and eth+IPv6+{TCP,UDP} packets. When the
    sysctl is enabled and a packet matches, it fills struct
    flow_keys byte-identically to the slow path and returns. On
    any miss it defers to the existing graph walk. Only the two
    standard dissectors are eligible (flow_keys_dissector and
    flow_keys_dissector_symmetric); custom dissectors defer. No
    new API, no ABI change, no header changes beyond one extern
    in <net/flow_dissector.h> for the static_branch key.

  - How it works. A single ethertype branch routes to a per-family
    helper. The helper does a fixed set of checks (version + IHL or
    extension headers, fragmentation, nexthdr), then writes
    key_control, key_basic, key_addrs, and key_ports from fixed
    offsets. Total +257 lines:
      net/core/flow_dissector.c                +217
      include/net/flow_dissector.h               +8
      net/core/sysctl_net_core.c                 +8
      Documentation/admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst  +24
    Fall-through cost on a miss is one ethertype switch
    (~5 cycles). BPF flow_dissector override semantics are
    preserved — the fast-path runs only after the existing BPF
    check decides no BPF program is loaded for this netns.

  - Why static_branch_likely (not _unlikely). Operators who flip
    this sysctl have opted into the fast-path because they want
    it fast; static_branch_likely keeps the fast-path body inline
    in the hot text section, matching the layout the microbench
    measured against. The trade is one forward JMP per dissector
    call in the default-off case (one not-taken JMP, cheap) vs. a
    cold-section detour per call in the enabled case if we had
    used _unlikely.

  - Why it is faster (sysctl=1). The slow-path graph walk
    dispatches through per-protocol function pointers and a
    linear lookup_node table per layer. For the common shapes the
    fast-path bypasses all of that.

    Operator-facing result, real workload (iperf3 -P 16 TCP/IPv4
    through sch_cake on a 25 GbE mlx5_core pair, same kernel,
    sysctl flipped at runtime, 60 s each):

      sysctl=0           sysctl=1           delta
      --------           --------           -----
      16.393 Gbit/s      16.723 Gbit/s     +2.0 %

    The fast-path saves roughly 5 ns of dissector cost per
    packet. At 25 GbE elephant-TCP rates that translates into
    the +2.0 % bulk throughput shown above. The per-packet
    saving is fixed, so it represents a larger relative share of
    the per-packet CPU budget at higher pps (smaller packets, DDoS
    workloads, RSS scatter) and at higher link rates -- we expect
    the relative gain to grow at 50/100 GbE, but we do not have
    that hardware on the testbed so we are not making a claim.

    On the i40e 10 GbE pair the link saturates at ~9.4 Gbit/s in
    both modes; the dissector saving shows up as CPU headroom
    rather than throughput. Retransmit rates are equivalent
    between sysctl=0 and sysctl=1 on both NICs, validating the
    byte-exact contract end-to-end (no flow-hash scatter).

    The +2.0 % reproduces across the kernel default fq_codel
    (+2.4 %), Eric Dumazet's fq (+1.0 %), and even noqueue
    (+0.8 %, no qdisc dissect at all -- residual saving comes
    from skb_get_hash, RPS/RFS, etc.). So the benefit is not
    cake-specific: roughly 60 % comes from qdisc classify and
    40 % from non-qdisc dissector callers in this workload. See
    perf-results/2026-06-07-series3-gated-ab/ in the project
    repo for the per-qdisc table.

    Isolated dissector cost (userspace microbench, libflowdis
    port, synthetic eth+IPv4+TCP, fast-path always hits, 10 M
    iterations, gcc -O3):

      uarch          host                           baseline  patched  delta
      -----          ----                           --------  -------  -----
      Zen 2          Threadripper PRO 3945WX  N=10  12.44 ns   6.56 ns -47.3%
      Skylake-deriv  Intel i9-10885H (t)      N=10  10.61 ns   5.62 ns -47.0%
      Zen 1          Ryzen 5 PRO 2400G        N=5   20.50 ns  20.53 ns noise
      Haswell-ULT    Intel Celeron 2955U      N=10  35.94 ns  17.97 ns -50.0%

    Both modern uarchs (Zen 2, Intel Skylake-deriv) land at ~47%
    on the always-hit synthetic case. The Intel data points
    confirm the speedup is not Zen-specific. On Zen 1 the saving
    is below the clock_gettime resolution at p50 (visible at
    p10); cycles are saved either way. The userspace microbench
    is the upper bound — it removes everything except the
    dissector cost. The +2.0 % macro number above is the realistic
    end-to-end result on a representative TCP workload.

  - When does this help in practice? Per-packet cost model
    (operator-facing reference for "should I enable this sysctl?"):

    The headline -47 to -55 % on synthetic always-hit traffic is
    the upper bound. Real workloads mix eligible
    (eth + IPv4/IPv6 + TCP/UDP, IHL=5, no fragmentation) and
    non-eligible (encap, VLAN, ICMP, fragments, IP options, etc.)
    shapes. Per-packet cost-benefit depends on the
    fast-path-eligible fraction.

    At sysctl=0 (default), the call site is a static_branch
    NOP-skip. Every packet takes the slow path; no behavioural
    or performance difference vs the unpatched kernel.

    At sysctl=1, the dispatcher attempts the fast-path on every
    packet. The dispatcher is designed to bail at the first
    failing check (`unlikely()` hints on every miss path):

      bail point                          | typical shapes that hit
      ------------------------------------|-----------------------------
      non-IP ethertype (switch default)   | ARP, VLAN, PPPoE, MPLS
      IP header length check              | truncated IP
      IPv4 version + IHL check (*iph!=0x45)| IPv4 with options
      IPv4 fragmentation check            | IPv4 fragments
      IPv4 protocol != TCP/UDP            | ICMP, AH, ESP, GRE inner
      IPv6 next-header != TCP/UDP         | ICMPv6, IPv6 ext headers, SRv6
      fast-path body completes (hit)      | eth+IPv4/IPv6+TCP/UDP, IHL=5,
                                          |   no fragments

    On a hit, the slow path is SKIPPED entirely — the headline
    cycle saving. On a miss, the slow path runs as before, plus
    the bounded bail-out cost.

    Net effect across N packets with hit fraction p:

      delta_per_packet = dispatcher_overhead  -  p * fast_path_savings

    Measured on Cortex-A76 (Pi 5, 2.4 GHz, userspace libflowdis):

      fast_path body              ~ 9 ns / pkt (always-hit synthetic)
      slow_path baseline          ~19 ns / pkt (always-hit synthetic)
      fast_path savings on hit    ~10 ns / pkt   (= 24 cycles)
      dispatcher_overhead on miss ~ 0.3 - 0.9 ns / pkt (measured
                                  across 34 non-eligible PCAPs in
                                  data/pcaps/ — covers ICMP,
                                  fragments, VLAN, QinQ, GRE,
                                  GRE-within-GRE, VXLAN, IPIP, L2TP,
                                  6in4, 6to4, SRv6 family)

    Cross-uarch dispatcher_overhead on miss (key parameter for
    breakeven on encap-heavy workloads):

      uarch                              | dispatcher_overhead
      -----------------------------------|--------------------
      Intel Comet Lake-H i9-10885H (OoO) | ~0.2 ns / pkt
      Cortex-A76 (Pi 5, OoO, 2.4 GHz)    | ~0.3 - 0.9 ns / pkt
      Cortex-A53 (Pi 3, in-order, 1.2 GHz)| ~6.7 - 9.9 ns / pkt

    The Cortex-A53 number is ~14-27x the Cortex-A76 number, while
    the body cost ratio (patched / baseline single-shape) is only
    ~6x. An in-order pipeline pays disproportionately more per
    dispatcher branch chain than an out-of-order one, because the
    dispatcher is exactly the workload pattern (short dependent
    load+compare chain) that OoO execution covers best.

    Breakeven hit fraction p ≈ 10-20 % on Cortex-A76 / x86; on
    Cortex-A53 the breakeven shifts higher — closer to p ≈ 50 %
    — because the dispatcher_overhead/fast_path_savings ratio is
    larger. Above breakeven, sysctl=1 wins on average. Below, it's
    a per-call cost on the non-matching majority. The order of
    magnitude is stable on OoO hardware (numerator and denominator
    both scale with core speed); on in-order ARM the operator
    should be more conservative.

    Operator guidance:

      Workload predominantly eth+IPv4/v6+TCP/UDP (datacenter
      east-west, HTTP edge fleets, database tiers, bulk file
      transfer, public-cloud guest VPC north-south):
        --> sysctl=1 wins

      Workload predominantly encapsulated or non-TCP/UDP
      (ISP CPE with PPPoE backhaul, VXLAN/Geneve overlays, GRE
      tunnels, ICMP-heavy monitoring, MPLS edge, IPSEC pass-
      through, anti-DDoS scrubbers):
        --> leave sysctl=0 (default)

      Small in-order ARM hosts (Cortex-A53, Cortex-A55,
      Cortex-A7, Raspberry Pi 3 / Pi Zero 2W / many low-end
      ARM CPE and edge appliances):
        --> the dispatcher_overhead/fast_path_savings ratio is
            ~10x worse than on OoO cores; the breakeven hit
            fraction shifts up to ~50 %. Operators with mixed
            traffic on these CPUs should default to sysctl=0
            unless they have measured their workload as
            >50 % fast-path-eligible. Bare-eth TCP/UDP-only
            fleets on these CPUs still benefit; everyone else
            should leave it off.

      Mixed workloads: the patches expose this on a per-host
      basis via the sysctl — measure your hit fraction against
      the breakeven for your uarch (~10-20 % on OoO, ~50 % on
      in-order ARM) and decide. The cost is per-packet, so the
      decision is independent of bandwidth.

    Numbers above are from
    perf-results/2026-06-10-series3-non-fast-path/ (Cortex-A76,
    34 single-shape non-fast-path PCAPs),
    perf-results/2026-06-10-series3-mixed-traffic/ (Cortex-A76,
    real workload PCAPs), and
    perf-results/2026-06-10-series3-pi3-pcap-microbench/ (Cortex-A53
    cross-uarch confirmation, 7-PCAP subset) in
    https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2/tree/main/perf-results/.

Output byte-equivalence is enforced by field-by-field matching of
the slow-path writes. A kernel selftest for byte-by-byte
comparison is planned for v2.

Tested
======

  - scripts/checkpatch.pl --strict: 0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 checks
    per patch.
  - W=1 build clean per patch.
  - sparse (master) and smatch (1.74) clean on
    net/core/flow_dissector.c.
  - Five-host testbed:
    * Pair 1: hp2 (gen) <-> hp5 (DUT), Intel X710 10 GbE i40e
    * Pair 2: hp1 (gen) <-> hp3 (DUT), Mellanox CX-4 Lx 25 GbE
      (Zen 1, Ryzen 5 PRO 2400G, 4c/8t)
    * t: Intel Comet Lake-H i9-10885H (Skylake-derivative)
      microbench-only host, WiFi-only.
  - 16 macro cells under the always-on draft (iperf3 + iperf2 x
    IPv4/IPv6 x TCP/UDP x 2 pairs, 60 s each, sch_cake
    triple-isolate, tuned TCP). All cells pass; no regression vs
    the series 1+2 B.1 24h baseline on mlx5. These results carry
    over to the gated kernel as the sysctl=1 data point — the
    gated path produces the same code as the always-on draft.
  - Gated kernel A/B verification (2026-06-07), 60 s iperf3
    -P 16 -t 60 per cell, sch_cake triple-isolate on each DUT:

      pair         sysctl=0           sysctl=1           delta
      ----         --------           --------           -----
      mlx5 25 GbE  16.393 Gbit/s      16.723 Gbit/s     +2.0 %
      i40e 10 GbE   9.409 Gbit/s       9.409 Gbit/s     0 (link-saturated)

    sysctl=0 matches or slightly exceeds the unpatched-kernel
    baseline on both NICs (no regression). sysctl=1 lands at the
    top of the prior Phase 4 range. Retransmit rates are
    equivalent between sysctl=0 and sysctl=1 on each pair, which
    validates the byte-exact contract end-to-end (no flow-hash
    scatter from broken dissector output). 0 cake drops, 0 cake
    overlimits, 0 new dmesg WARN/BUG/oops across all four runs.
  - 24h soaks, both pairs in parallel, under the prior always-on
    draft (kept as the sysctl=1 24h data point):
      i40e iperf3: 279 runs, 9.4114 Gbit/s mean, CV 0.011 %,
                   drift -0.000 %, 0 cake drops.
      mlx5 iperf2: 284 runs, 16.258 Gbit/s mean, CV 1.68 %,
                   drift -0.504 %, 7 cake drops, +11 MB RSS.
    0 new dmesg WARN/BUG/oops on either host throughout.

  - ARM/Broadcom validation (2026-06-09), third CPU vendor in the
    test fleet, Cortex-A76 + A72 uarch families:

      * Build + boot: patches apply cleanly against the
        nixos-raspberrypi linux_rpi5 and linux_rpi4 6.12.87
        kernels (no 6.12 context drift in the touched files).
        sysctl registered, default 0, dmesg clean of new
        WARN/BUG/oops on all three hosts (pi5-1, pi5-2, pi4-1).

      * TCP wire-rate cross-qdisc matrix on the Pi 5 pair
        (Cortex-A76 4c @ 2.4 GHz, 1 GbE switched, -P 16 -t 30 s,
        cake/fq_codel/fq/noqueue each tested):

           sysctl=0     sysctl=1     delta
           936.20       936.16       -0.004 %   cake
           936.15       936.21       +0.006 %   fq_codel
           936.25       936.18       -0.007 %   fq
           936.16       936.18       +0.002 %   noqueue
                                                (Mbit/s, both modes)

        All four qdiscs produce link-saturated throughput with 0
        retransmits in both modes — no regression at sysctl=0
        on ARM, and the byte-exact contract holds end-to-end
        (no flow-hash scatter from broken dissector output).

      * UDP -b 0 -l 64 -P 8 (PPS-limited, 20 s, N=3 per mode):
        sysctl=0 162.8 Mbit/s mean (160.4 - 164.2)
        sysctl=1 153.8 Mbit/s mean (151.5 - 156.9)
                                          delta -5.5 %

        Every sysctl=1 run lies below every sysctl=0 run, so the
        delta is signal not noise. This is an unexpected
        ARM-specific finding — Cortex-A76 sees a small UDP
        small-packet regression at sysctl=1 while x86 does not.
        Working hypotheses (none verified): dispatcher branch
        chain predicting worse on A76, 16 KiB page boundary
        effects on the new code, or fast-path .text layout
        crossing a cache line that the slow path didn't.
        Default-off remains free, so operators who don't opt in
        see no impact; we plan to investigate the layout fix
        in v2.

    Details: perf-results/2026-06-09-series3-arm-ab/ in the
    project repo.

      * Cross-uarch kernel macro from pi5-2 (A76 sender) to
        pi4-1 (A72) and pi3-1 (A53, 100 Mbit USB-eth, in-order
        pipeline — the slowest receiver in the fleet, intentionally
