        whole matrix, well below cell-to-cell noise.

      * Mixed-packet kernel macro via tcpreplay from pi5-2 to
        pi4-1 / pi3-1. Replay rates 500 Mbit/s (pi4-1) and 80
        Mbit/s (pi3-1) against rewritten broad-coverage.pcap
        (5200-packet diverse non-eligible mix) and
        vxlan-k8s-pure.pcap (20 000-packet VXLAN-encap K8s mix).
        Receiver-side mean CPU% across all 4 cores over 20 s
        replay window, A/B sysctl:

          recv    pcap             cpu=0    cpu=1    Δ pp
          pi4-1   broad-coverage   16.54 %  15.86 %  -0.68
                  vxlan-k8s-pure   17.18 %  17.18 %  +0.00
          pi3-1   broad-coverage   14.53 %  14.10 %  -0.42
                  vxlan-k8s-pure   12.26 %  12.15 %  -0.11

        All four cells: |Δ| ≤ 0.7 pp, both directions of sign —
        within mpstat noise on a heavy-rx-drop softirq workload.
        The userspace PCAP microbench's +10-15 % per-call
        dispatcher cost on Cortex-A53 (see
        perf-results/2026-06-10-series3-pi3-pcap-microbench/)
        does not translate to a measurable kernel macro signal
        because the dissector is only ~5-10 % of total kernel
        receive cost — the relative microbench cost compresses
        to fraction-of-a-percent at macro.

        Details: perf-results/2026-06-10-series3-cross-uarch-perf/
        and perf-results/2026-06-10-series3-tcpreplay-mixed/.

      * 10 h sustained-load ARM cross-uarch soak (2026-06-10/11):
        pi5-1<->pi5-2 (A76<->A76), pi5-2<->pi4-1 (A76<->A72) and
        pi5-2<->pi3-1 (A76<->A53) over 1 GbE switched + 100 Mbit
        USB-eth, 6 x iperf3 TCP + 2 x iperf2 TCP + 2 x tcpreplay
        vxlan-k8s-pure, 60 min each, sysctl=0 vs sysctl=1 paired
        cells. 1 Hz vcgencmd telemetry on both ends; new fans
        installed on all four Pis.

        All 10 cells thermal clean -- max receiver temp 65.3 C
        (pi5-2), well below the Pi 5 soft-temp limit; no current
        throttle bits set in any sample.  Per-cell drift
        <= 0.08 %/hour; CV 0.47-1.00 % on the 1 GbE cells.

        Throughput delta sysctl=0 -> sysctl=1: <= +/- 0.001 %
        across every cell (every TCP cell link-saturated in both
        modes; retransmits identical within USB-Ethernet jitter).

        Receiver-CPU% delta at sysctl=1 over 1-h sustained load:
          A76 receiver:    -0.05 pp (within noise)
          A72 receiver:    -1.30 pp (-2.0 % of rx budget) --
                           per-packet fast-path body savings
                           visible where the slower receive-side
                           core makes the dissector a larger
                           fraction of rx cost
          A53 receiver:    -0.64 pp on tcpreplay-vxlan
                           +0.01 pp on iperf3 TCP

        No regression on any receiver under sustained load. The
        A72 cell is the first kernel-macro signal of the
        fast-path body savings on real wire-rate traffic; the
        per-packet cycle saving manifests as a measurable CPU%
        reduction on the in-between uarch where the dissector
        is large enough to matter but cache thrash isn't yet
        masking it.

        Reproducible via `nix run .#series3-soak`.

        Details: perf-results/2026-06-10-series3-soak-10h/.

This is sent as RFC for feedback on:

  - The dispatch shape (single ethertype switch with per-family
    helpers).
  - The byte-exact output rule (vs the cheaper alternative of
    skipping "cold" slot writes that no current consumer reads).
  - The dissector-identity check (only the two standard
    dissectors, vs a broader rule based on key-set subset).
  - The sysctl name (net.core.flow_dissector_fastpath) and gate
    shape (static_branch_likely + proc_do_static_key). The
    default-off / opt-in choice matches the established pattern
    for new flow_dissector behaviour that operators opt into for
    performance; if a different default or gate mechanism is
    preferred we are happy to respin.

A single-patch alternative form of the same code (skeleton + IPv4
+ IPv6 squashed into one commit) is also available for reviewers
who prefer a single diff to read end-to-end:
https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2/tree/main/kernel-patches/series3-flowdis-fastpath/v2-experiment

